<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xml:lang="en"><generator uri="https://jekyllrb.com/" version="4.4.1">Jekyll</generator><link href="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/feed.xml" rel="self" type="application/atom+xml"/><link href="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" hreflang="en"/><updated>2026-10-16T21:45:26-04:00</updated><id>https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/feed.xml</id><title type="html">blank</title><subtitle>Chonghe Jiang is a PhD student at MIT IDSS studying optimization, reinforcement learning, and LLM post-training.</subtitle><entry><title type="html">Revisiting Autoresearch for Scientific Discovery</title><link href="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/autoresearch-as-optimization/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Revisiting Autoresearch for Scientific Discovery"/><published>2026-10-15T00:00:00-04:00</published><updated>2026-10-15T00:00:00-04:00</updated><id>https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/autoresearch-as-optimization</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/autoresearch-as-optimization/"><![CDATA[<h2 id="methods">Methods</h2> <figure class="ar-figure ar-map ar-evolution" id="ar-map" aria-labelledby="ar-map-title"> <div class="ar-figure-heading"><h3 id="ar-map-title">Same loop. Different update rules.</h3></div> <div class="ar-map-controls ar-grouped-controls" hidden="" role="group" aria-label="Papers grouped by contribution"> <button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-components="all" data-title="Shared loop" data-description="Candidates and feedback carry progress around the loop. Most methods change search state; MIRA-AC also trains the decision policy.">Overview</button> <div class="ar-method-group" role="group" aria-labelledby="ar-group-state"><span class="ar-group-label" id="ar-group-state">State &amp; context</span><div class="ar-group-papers"> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="propose memory" data-title="Fork-and-Flush" data-description="Fork the workspace into fresh-context continuations, then continue the best branch." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07447">Fork-and-Flush</button> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent memory" data-title="Scaling Discovery Requires Forgetting" data-description="Restart the team from the best candidate after clearing sessions and the other candidate history." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20563">Forgetting</button> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent propose memory" data-title="Stateless Language Agents" data-description="The harness retains evidence and rebuilds each context; an Advisor assigns experiments to Workers." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07625">SLA</button> </div></div> <div class="ar-method-group" role="group" aria-labelledby="ar-group-decisions"><span class="ar-group-label" id="ar-group-decisions">Search decisions</span><div class="ar-group-papers"> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent" data-title="Contrastive Concept-Tree Search" data-description="Use concepts associated with strong and weak programs to guide parent selection and edits." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03132">CCTS</button> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent" data-title="MIRA-AC" data-description="Learn research decisions from their downstream returns while keeping the executor fixed." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02525">MIRA-AC</button> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent propose evaluate" data-title="IdeaScientist" data-description="GRPO trains gap finding, innovation and report writing; a fixed orchestrator coordinates research proposal generation." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04074">IdeaScientist</button> </div></div> <div class="ar-method-group" role="group" aria-labelledby="ar-group-evaluation"><span class="ar-group-label" id="ar-group-evaluation">Evaluation</span><div class="ar-group-papers"> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent evaluate" data-title="DuoDiscover" data-description="Discover targets and cheap proxy evaluators together, and confirm gains with true evaluations." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17184">DuoDiscover</button> </div></div> </div> <div class="ar-evo-canvas"> <svg class="ar-evo-edges ar-evo-wide" viewBox="0 0 720 322" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><defs><marker id="ar-arrow-wide" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar-arrow-wide)"><path d="M336 72H382"/><path d="M552 144V176"/><path d="M384 250H338"/><path d="M168 178V146"/></g></svg> <svg class="ar-evo-edges ar-evo-narrow" viewBox="0 0 340 406" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><defs><marker id="ar-arrow-narrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="5" markerHeight="5" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar-arrow-narrow)"><path d="M162 95H176"/><path d="M259 190V214"/><path d="M178 311H164"/><path d="M81 216V192"/></g></svg> <ol class="ar-evo-nodes"> <li class="ar-evo-node ar-evo-parent" data-component="parent"><span class="ar-number">01</span><strong>Select &amp; adapt</strong><p class="ar-node-copy">Choose a parent and use feedback to steer the next attempt.</p></li> <li class="ar-evo-node ar-evo-propose" data-component="propose"><span class="ar-number">02</span><strong>Generate</strong><p class="ar-node-copy">Revise a candidate or carry out an investigation.</p></li> <li class="ar-evo-node ar-evo-evaluate" data-component="evaluate"><span class="ar-number">03</span><strong>Evaluate</strong><p class="ar-node-copy">Measure quality and correctness.</p></li> <li class="ar-evo-node ar-evo-memory" data-component="memory"><span class="ar-number">04</span><strong>Select &amp; remember</strong><p class="ar-node-copy">Retain useful candidates and evidence for the next round.</p></li> </ol> </div> <div class="ar-map-detail" hidden="" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"><span class="ar-training-label">Search-state updates; policy training is optional</span><strong>Shared loop</strong><p>Candidates and feedback carry progress around the loop. Most methods change search state; MIRA-AC also trains the decision policy.</p><a hidden="">Read the paper ↗</a></div> <figcaption>Select a paper to see how it changes the loop.</figcaption> </figure> <h2 id="experiments">Models &amp; Tasks</h2> <p>Eval time covers one candidate’s execution and scoring, excluding LLM generation. <strong>Light</strong> means short checks; <strong>Heavy</strong> means minutes or longer. <strong>NR</strong> means the paper does not report the time; <strong>†</strong> marks a qualitative estimate from the workload. A cheap final check can still follow an expensive solver run.</p> <div class="ar-study-table-wrap ar-eval-table" role="region" aria-label="Models, tasks and evaluation cost by paper" tabindex="0"> <table> <thead> <tr> <th style="text-align: left">Paper</th> <th style="text-align: left">Models</th> <th style="text-align: left">Tasks</th> <th style="text-align: left">Eval time &amp; cost</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.07447#S5.SS1">Fork-and-Flush</a></td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>GPT-5.6-terra</strong> (high); <strong>GPT-5.5</strong> (extra-high) on VLIW and tinyCIFAR. Four branches via Copilot CLI.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>Frontier-CS:</strong> polyomino, job-shop, AHC014, p150, p171, sphere packing, relay layout, LWE recovery, LLM-SQL.<br/><br/><strong>EdgeBench:</strong> order-add, Borden inversion.<br/><br/><strong>Engineering:</strong> VLIW, tinyCIFAR.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>NR</strong> per evaluation for all 13 tasks.<br/><br/>VLIW scoring: <strong>Light†</strong>. tinyCIFAR retraining: <strong>Heavy†</strong>. Other tasks: <strong>Variable</strong>, depending on the solver and test suite.<br/><br/>The hours in <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.07447#A2">Table 4</a> are whole-search times.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.20563#page=5">Forgetting</a></td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>DeepSeek V4.1 Flash / Pi</strong>. Four communicating agents, reset from the best candidate.</td> <td style="text-align: left">AHC014; Frontier-CS Q171; VLIW; nanochat training.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>nanochat:</strong> <strong>5 min training</strong> on one H100, plus validation; <strong>Heavy</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>AHC014 / Q171:</strong> NR for the 30 / 75-case suites; <strong>Variable</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>VLIW:</strong> NR; <strong>Light†</strong> scoring. <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.20563#page=13">Task details</a>.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.07625#S4.SS1">SLA</a></td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>GPT-5.5 / Codex</strong> or <strong>Opus 4.8 / Claude Code</strong>. Stateless Advisor and Workers.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>FrontierSWE:</strong> libexpat → assembly, Git → Zig, Dart → Haskell, Lua compiler.<br/><br/><strong>Kernels:</strong> VLIW; SOL-Exec #1, #58, #210.<br/><br/><strong>Cryptanalysis:</strong> Structured-LWE.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>NR</strong> per evaluation for all tasks.<br/><br/>VLIW scoring: <strong>Light†</strong>. FrontierSWE, SOL-Exec and LWE: <strong>Variable</strong>, depending on builds, test suites and solver runtime.<br/><br/>DuoDiscover’s SOL-Exec timings below use a different setup.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2602.03132v1#S5">CCTS</a></td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>Gemini 2.0 Flash</strong> generates programs and extracts concepts; a statistical model selects parents.</td> <td style="text-align: left">Circle packing; arithmetic Kakeya; Heilbronn’s triangle; squares in a square; synthetic discovery.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>NR</strong> for all five settings.<br/><br/>Final numerical or synthetic score checks: <strong>Light†</strong>. The full evaluation also executes the generated search program; its runtime is unspecified.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02525#S6">MIRA / MIRA-AC</a></td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>Qwen3.6-27B</strong> decision policy and shared critic; fixed curator and Codex executor. Untrained MIRA also uses <strong>GPT-5.5</strong>.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>RL:</strong> SRBench, Physics Discovery, BNLearn, HillClimbBench-CPU.<br/><br/><strong>Untrained:</strong> IMOProofBench, neural-architecture research.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>NR</strong> per evaluation throughout.<br/><br/>SRBench / BNLearn scoring: <strong>Light†</strong>. Physics simulation and IMO judging: <strong>Variable</strong>. HillClimbBench-CPU / architecture trials: <strong>Heavy†</strong>, requiring model training. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.02525#A2">Evaluation protocols</a>.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.04074#S5">IdeaScientist</a></td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>Qwen3.6-27B</strong>, with GRPO-trained ideation roles. Base variants: <strong>Gemma-4-31B</strong>, <strong>GPT-OSS-120B</strong>.</td> <td style="text-align: left">Literature-grounded proposals from Svalbard Idea Vault; novelty and proposal-quality assessment.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>NR</strong> per proposal; <strong>judge-dependent</strong>. Retrieval and LLM review, with no execution of the proposed scientific experiments.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.17184#S3">DuoDiscover</a></td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>Opus 5 / Claude Code</strong>, <strong>Sonnet 5</strong> helper, four Workers. Additional runs: <strong>Gemini 3.8 Flash / Antigravity</strong>.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>Repositories:</strong> FormulaCode, SWE-fficiency.<br/><br/><strong>Kernels:</strong> SOL-Exec, JAXBench, TPU-Inference, FA4.<br/><br/><strong>Case studies:</strong> vLLM, FreeToken, SQLite, zstd, CPU prefetchers.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><strong>Full evaluation: Heavy.</strong><br/>FormulaCode: <strong>7 min–6.8 h</strong>.<br/>SWE-fficiency: <strong>15 min</strong> median (3–93).<br/>SOL-Exec: <strong>14 min</strong> median (2–60).<br/>JAXBench: <strong>7–32 min</strong>.<br/>TPU-Inference: <strong>1–8 min</strong>.<br/>FA4: <strong>4 min</strong>.<br/>vLLM / FreeToken: <strong>85 / 70 min</strong>.<br/>SQLite: <strong>10 min</strong>; zstd: <strong>15–25 min</strong>.<br/>Prefetchers: <strong>up to 28 h</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>Proxies: Light</strong>, median <strong>16 s</strong> on sampled SOL-Exec kernels and <strong>40 s</strong> on sampled SWE-fficiency tasks. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.17184#A2">Timings</a> · <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.17184#S4">Proxies</a>.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> </div>]]></content><author><name></name></author><summary type="html"><![CDATA[How research agents manage context, choose experiments, and evaluate progress.]]></summary></entry><entry><title type="html">Revisiting PPO for LLM Post-Training</title><link href="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/revisiting-ppo/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Revisiting PPO for LLM Post-Training"/><published>2026-10-14T00:00:00-04:00</published><updated>2026-10-14T00:00:00-04:00</updated><id>https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/revisiting-ppo</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/revisiting-ppo/"><![CDATA[<p>Recent PPO work revisits how trajectories are sampled, how the critic estimates returns, and how policy updates handle distribution mismatch. Mapping these changes onto the training loop makes the methods easier to compare.</p> <figure class="ppo-map" id="ppo-paper-map" aria-labelledby="ppo-map-title"> <div class="ppo-map-heading"><span class="note-label">From rollout to update</span><h3 id="ppo-map-title">Where recent papers change PPO</h3></div> <ol class="ppo-stages"> <li class="ppo-stage" data-stage="1"><div class="ppo-stage-top"><span class="ppo-stage-number">01</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6h7m-7 6h7m-7 6h7m4-12 5 6-5 6M12 12h8"/></svg></div><strong>Rollouts</strong><p>Generate trajectories</p><div class="ppo-stage-papers"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07508" data-paper="sao">SAO</a><a href="https://yifanzhang-pro.github.io/FlashREINFORCE/" data-paper="flash">FlashREINFORCE</a></div></li> <li class="ppo-stage" data-stage="2"><div class="ppo-stage-top"><span class="ppo-stage-number">02</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 19V5m0 14h16M8 15l4-5 4 2 4-7"/><circle cx="12" cy="10" r="1"/></svg></div><strong>Reward &amp; value</strong><p>Evaluate outcomes and prefixes</p><div class="ppo-stage-papers"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19295" data-paper="tempo">TEMPO</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.39247" data-paper="ac2">AC2</a></div></li> <li class="ppo-stage" data-stage="3"><div class="ppo-stage-top"><span class="ppo-stage-number">03</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 12h16M12 4v16m-5-5 5 5 5-5M7 9l5-5 5 5"/></svg></div><strong>Advantages</strong><p>Assign credit relative to a baseline</p><div class="ppo-stage-papers"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07508" data-paper="sao">SAO</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.39247" data-paper="ac2">AC2</a><a href="https://yifanzhang-pro.github.io/FlashREINFORCE/" data-paper="flash">FlashREINFORCE</a></div></li> <li class="ppo-stage" data-stage="4"><div class="ppo-stage-top"><span class="ppo-stage-number">04</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 7h13l-3-3m3 3-3 3M20 17H7l3-3m-3 3 3 3"/></svg></div><strong>Policy correction</strong><p>Account for distribution mismatch</p><div class="ppo-stage-papers"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20807" data-paper="centering">Score Centering</a><a href="https://github.com/areal-project/AReaL/blob/main/docs/en/best_practices/algo_perf.md" data-paper="decoupled">Decoupled PPO</a></div></li> <li class="ppo-stage" data-stage="5"><div class="ppo-stage-top"><span class="ppo-stage-number">05</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 18h4v-5H4zm6 0h4V9h-4zm6 0h4V5h-4z"/></svg></div><strong>Updates</strong><p>Optimize actor and critic</p><div class="ppo-stage-papers"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36802" data-paper="easy">EasyPPO</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07508" data-paper="sao">SAO</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19295" data-paper="tempo">TEMPO</a><a href="https://github.com/areal-project/AReaL/blob/main/docs/en/best_practices/algo_perf.md" data-paper="decoupled">Decoupled PPO</a></div></li> </ol> <div class="ppo-loop"><span aria-hidden="true">↶</span> Updated policy and value model feed the next round</div> <div class="ppo-map-interaction" hidden=""> <div class="ppo-paper-select" role="group" aria-label="Highlight a paper's contributions"><button type="button" data-paper="all" aria-pressed="true">All papers</button> <button type="button" data-paper="sao" data-stages="1 3 5" data-detail="Single-rollout asynchronous sampling, a learned value baseline, and strict double-sided token clipping." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07508" aria-pressed="false">SAO</button> <button type="button" data-paper="easy" data-stages="5" data-detail="Stabilize critic training with actor-only overlong filtering, noise-normalized regression, and smaller critic minibatches." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36802" aria-pressed="false">EasyPPO</button> <button type="button" data-paper="tempo" data-stages="2 5" data-detail="Use critic feedback for policy refinement on unlabeled questions, then recalibrate the critic on labeled data." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19295" aria-pressed="false">TEMPO</button> <button type="button" data-paper="ac2" data-stages="2 3" data-detail="Use generative critic estimates for action-chunk advantages and updates, gated by local readiness." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.39247" aria-pressed="false">AC2</button> <button type="button" data-paper="flash" data-stages="1 3" data-detail="Single-rollout asynchronous learning with batch centering replaces the learned value baseline. This is a critic-free alternative to PPO." data-url="https://yifanzhang-pro.github.io/FlashREINFORCE/" aria-pressed="false">FlashREINFORCE</button> <button type="button" data-paper="centering" data-stages="4" data-detail="Apply an additive correction for gradient drift from training–inference mismatch. Score here means the log-probability gradient." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20807" aria-pressed="false">Score Centering</button> <button type="button" data-paper="decoupled" data-stages="4 5" data-detail="Separate behavior-policy importance correction from clipping around a recent proximal policy." data-url="https://github.com/areal-project/AReaL/blob/main/docs/en/best_practices/algo_perf.md" aria-pressed="false">Decoupled PPO</button> </div> <div class="ppo-paper-detail" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"><span class="ppo-detail-name">Explore the loop</span><p>Select a paper to highlight the stages it changes.</p><a hidden="">Read the paper ↗</a></div> </div> <figcaption>Selected contributions across PPO and related methods. A paper can span several stages; FlashREINFORCE removes the critic.</figcaption> </figure> <h2 id="recent-work">Revisiting PPO</h2> <p>PPO gives the actor and critic different jobs. The actor learns which actions to make more likely. The critic estimates the return expected from the current prompt and generated prefix, providing a baseline for the actor’s update.</p> <aside class="ppo-math-block" aria-labelledby="ppo-components-title"> <h3 id="ppo-components-title">Two updates, different interventions</h3> <p>For actor $\pi_\theta$ and critic $V_\phi$, write $\psi_\theta=\nabla_\theta\log\pi_\theta(a\mid s)$. Their updates expose the main components:</p> \[\begin{aligned} g_{\mathrm{actor}}&amp;=\mathbb E_{\mathcal D}\!\left[m\,\hat A\,(w\psi_\theta-c(s))\right],\\[4pt] L_V&amp;=\mathbb E_{\mathcal D}\!\left[m^V\omega_x\,\ell(V_\phi(s),Y)\right]. \end{aligned}\] <p>$\mathcal D$: sampled trajectories · $m,m^V$: masks and aggregation · $\hat A$: advantage · $w$: probability correction · $c$: score correction · $\omega_x,Y$: critic weight and target. This is a component map: clipping enters through effective weights/masks; KL terms are omitted.</p> <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Papers mapped to mathematical components" tabindex="0"> <table> <thead> <tr> <th style="text-align: left">Work</th> <th style="text-align: left">Main intervention</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2607.07508v1">SAO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">$\mathcal D$: one rollout per prompt; $\hat A$: skip-observation GAE; $w$: strict token masking; more frequent critic updates.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://yifanzhang-pro.github.io/FlashREINFORCE/">FlashREINFORCE</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">$\hat A_i=R_i-\bar R_{\mathrm{batch}}$; single rollouts, sequence admission, per-trajectory averaging; no $L_V$.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.39247v2">AC2</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">$\hat A_i=v_i-\bar v$, $Y=\bar v$; critic-ready problems use $v_i=V(s\cdot c_i)$ for unfinished chunks.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2604.19295v1">TEMPO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">$\hat A_t=V(y_{\mathrm{complete}})-V(s_t)$; periodically recalibrate $V$ on labeled data.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.20807v1">Score Centering</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">$c(s)=\mathbb E_{a\sim\mu(\cdot\mid s)}[w\psi_\theta]$: center the weighted score under the sampler $\mu$.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://github.com/areal-project/AReaL/blob/main/docs/en/best_practices/algo_perf.md">Decoupled PPO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Separate $\nu/\mu$ for behavior correction from $\pi_\theta/\nu$ for PPO clipping; $\nu$ is a recent policy.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.36802v1">EasyPPO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">$\omega_x\propto1/\max(\hat\sigma_x,\varepsilon)$; $m^V$ retains truncated samples; smaller critic minibatches.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> </div> <p>For complete terminal-return PPO with $\gamma=\lambda=1$, $\hat A_t=R-V_{\mathrm{old}}(s_t)$. Reward centering changes $\hat A$; score centering changes the gradient term $c(s)$.</p> </aside> <p>These papers use the critic in different ways. A baseline reduces variance after we observe an outcome. A learned evaluator supplies feedback when a real outcome is unavailable or expensive. The second role asks much more of the model.</p> <h2 id="special">What makes TTT special?</h2> <h3 id="one-problem-many-attempts">One problem, many attempts</h3> <p>Here, <strong>sample = 1 at the task level</strong>. We adapt to one target problem, rather than training over a broad collection of unrelated questions. We can still generate many candidates and evaluate them on many cases. All those attempts serve the same search.</p> <p>This also changes the input distribution. Each successful revision becomes a possible starting point for the next attempt. The model learns while the problem in front of it changes.</p> <h3 id="we-keep-the-best-of-all-attempts">We keep the best of all attempts</h3> <p>At the end, we want the strongest verified solution found anywhere in the history. The objective is closer to:</p> \[\mathbb E\!\left[\max_{1\leq k\leq N} S(c_k)\right],\] <p>where later candidates can depend on earlier results. Repeatedly producing a good existing solution helps average reward but adds little to that maximum. We need enough reliability to keep the search moving, alongside a willingness to try changes that might fail.</p> <h3 id="discovery-needs-depth">Discovery needs depth</h3> <p>An experiment often reveals what to try next. Generating a large batch before receiving feedback delays that process: every candidate in the batch starts from the same available history.</p> <p>With a fixed candidate budget, larger batches leave fewer rounds to learn from the results. Smaller batches give later attempts more opportunities to build on earlier discoveries.</p> <figure class="concept-figure depth-figure"> <span class="note-label">The same 64 executions, different feedback schedules</span> <div class="depth-row"><div class="depth-label"><strong>Batch 32</strong><small>2 feedback rounds</small></div><div class="depth-rounds depth-two"><span>Round 1</span><span>Round 2</span></div></div> <div class="depth-row"><div class="depth-label"><strong>Batch 8</strong><small>8 feedback rounds</small></div><div class="depth-rounds depth-eight"><span>1</span><span>2</span><span>3</span><span>4</span><span>5</span><span>6</span><span>7</span><span>8</span></div></div> <figcaption>A fixed-budget illustration: smaller batches let later candidates use feedback from more rounds.</figcaption> </figure> <p>Parallel exploration still matters, and smaller batches can make updates noisier. The question is how much breadth we can afford before sacrificing useful feedback depth.</p>]]></content><author><name></name></author><summary type="html"><![CDATA[Recent approaches to rollouts, value estimation, and policy updates, with implications for test-time discovery.]]></summary></entry></feed>